{"timestamp":"2026-04-24T12:30:57.425Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"**Findings**\n\n1. No major behavioral change: the current push-time sync already mostly does “reset to upstream, then replay local-only commits.”\nAt [push_common.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common.go:284), `fetchAndRebaseSessionsCommon` fetches the remote tip, finds the merge base, and if local is ahead/diverged it collects local-only non-merge commits and replays them onto the fetched remote tip via `cherryPickOnto` ([push_common.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common.go:374), [metadata_reconcile.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/metadata_reconcile.go:407)). So switching to an explicit “point local ref at upstream first” model would mostly be a refactoring of the current semantics, not a new reconciliation strategy.\n\n2. The main correctness requirement is not the reset; it is which commits get replayed.\nThe implementation intentionally replays only `local \\ remote` non-merge commits (`git rev-list ... --no-merges`) ([push_common.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common.go:430)). If you instead reset to upstream and cherry-pick the entire local lineage, you would reapply shared history and old merge effects, which can resurrect checkpoint shards that the remote later deleted. That regression is covered by [push_common_test.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common_test.go:629).\n\n3. Merge commits must stay excluded.\n`cherryPickOnto` computes each commit as a first-parent tree delta. That works for linear commits, but replaying merge commits would reapply changes imported through non-first-parent history and corrupt the reconciled tree. The current code explicitly avoids that ([push_common.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common.go:436)).\n\n4. A literal git `reset` is safe only if it means “move the metadata ref,” not “reset the checked-out worktree.”\nThe current implementation updates `refs/heads/entire/checkpoints/v1` directly with `repo.Storer.SetReference(...)` ([push_common.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/push_common.go:364), [metadata_reconcile.go](/home/coder/git/entireio/cli-sign/cmd/entire/cli/strategy/metadata_reconcile.go:175)). That is safe because this branch is not the working branch. Replacing it with an actual `git reset --hard` would be the wrong tool and risks touching worktree state.\n\n**Impact**\n\nIf you mean “set the local checkpoint-branch ref to the fetched upstream tip, then cherry-pick only local-only non-merge commits on top,” the impact is small and mostly positive in clarity. It matches what the code already does for both normal divergence and disconnected-history repair. You would still rewrite local metadata-branch commit SHAs, but that already happens today whenever reconciliation cherry-picks.\n\nThe real constraints are:\n\n- Use the fetched upstream ref, not stale tracking state.\n- Replay only `local-only` commits since merge-base.\n- Skip merge commits.\n- Preserve deletions as well as additions/modifications.\n\nIf those rules hold, resetting to upstream first is effectively equivalent to the current approach.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
